WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:03.190
BETH: It’s common for some
individuals on the autistic spectrum

00:00:03.190 --> 00:00:04.779
to behave in challenging ways.

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:08.629
Once the reasons behind this are
known, and there usually is a reason

00:00:08.629 --> 00:00:11.930
for behaviour that challenges, there
are ways that this can be managed to

00:00:11.930 --> 00:00:13.879
the benefit of everyone concerned.

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:18.439
When individuals with autism can manage
their own behaviour, they can learn

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:20.380
how to get along better with others.

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:25.120
JOE: Some individuals with autism might
refuse or ignore requests from others

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:30.340
and behave in a way which is socially
inappropriate such as disrobing, showing

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:32.160
aggression or injuring themselves.

00:00:32.929 --> 00:00:36.890
This behaviour may challenge the feelings
and thoughts of others they interact with.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:41.850
When those who interact with individuals
with autism understand why they behave

00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:46.350
this way, such as having trouble with
verbal and non-verbal communication and

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:50.779
the frustration it can cause, they are
more likely to respond more positively.

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:56.630
If they are aware of what might trigger
the individual with autism, they

00:00:56.630 --> 00:01:00.320
can try to alleviate any stressors
which could develop into triggers

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:02.250
to behaviour that challenges others.

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:08.870
BETH: Challenging behaviour is in itself
not always a problem for an individual

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:13.784
with autism, as it can often be a way of
them gaining satisfaction and control over

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:18.539
a situation, however it is challenging
to those around them, as it does not

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:22.870
fit with what is recognised generally
as ‘normal’ or acceptable behaviour.

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:27.589
JOE: Sometime a particular person is
may become a target for behaviour that

00:01:27.589 --> 00:01:32.670
challenges, it could be a peer, a family
member, a teacher or a support worker.

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:37.070
BETH: Whoever it is, it is important to
think about what the trigger might be.

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:40.889
It may be the clothes they wear
such as the pattern, texture or

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:45.109
colour, it may be their perfume is
overwhelming to the individual or

00:01:45.110 --> 00:01:49.049
maybe the person is associated with
a distressing place or activity.

00:01:49.749 --> 00:01:52.380
JOE: Maybe they don’t like the fact
that the person spends too much

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:56.640
time with others, in their eyes, as
they want them solely to themselves,

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:00.560
or they may simply clash with
the person for no obvious reason.

00:02:01.469 --> 00:02:05.489
This can be remedied if the person
is a support worker as other people

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:09.430
could replace them but if it is a
family member it is more of a problem.

